statement:
Let: \[ B_{F,\Gamma}=\operatorname{Min}_{\prec}(V_F\cap\operatorname{Real}_R(\Gamma)). \] CU distinguishes: \[ 	ext{strong warrant: } B_{F,\Gamma}
eq\varnothing \land B_{F,\Gamma}\subseteq\llbracket\varphirbracket_R, \] \[ 	ext{admissibility: } B_{F,\Gamma}
eq\varnothing \land B_{F,\Gamma}\cap\llbracket\varphirbracket_R
eq\varnothing, \] \[ 	ext{exclusion: } B_{F,\Gamma}
eq\varnothing \land B_{F,\Gamma}\cap\llbracket\varphirbracket_R=\varnothing. \] **Mere admissibility** is admissibility without strong warrant. **Infeasibility** obtains when \(V_F\cap\operatorname{Real}_R(\Gamma)=\varnothing\). **Underdetermination** has two forms. **Formal non-attainment** occurs when the premise-compatible set is nonempty but has no minimal elements under the declared ordering. **Model-adequacy abstention** occurs before a best-set verdict is issued because the represented facts, affected-party scope, options, floors, horizon, observation model, or ordering remain unspecified, contested, or inadequate. Ties or incomparability within an attained best set do not themselves create underdetermination; they are handled by strong warrant, mere admissibility, or exclusion.
